+# System Interaction and Dependency Map
+
+## Core Spine
+
+```text
+world hazard, conflict or public claim
+→ observer evidence and incident uncertainty
+→ local unit charters, capability and readiness
+→ mobilization request and household cost
+→ temporary coalition and delegated authority graph
+→ logistics, rescue, defense and civilian movement
+→ refusal, amendment, command succession and oversight
+→ demobilization, recovery, accountability and repair
+→ unit memory, legitimacy change and future readiness
+```
+
+# Required Dependencies
+
+## World and Chronicle truth
+
+Incidents, damage, people, communications, claims, orders, evidence, and recovery remain source-chain grounded.
+
+## Population and households
+
+Reserve participants come from workplaces, families, care systems, professions, and local politics.
+
+## Institutions and charters
+
+Units, compacts, carriers, courts, councils, and strategic services possess versioned authority and succession.
+
+## Logistics and infrastructure
+
+Protection requires routes, depots, vehicles, atmosphere, water, medicine, power, repair stock, thermal capacity, and receiving facilities.
+
+## Professional simulation
+
+Medical, engineering, navigation, evidence, translation, and safety authority remain distinct from general command.
+
+## Warfare and threat ecology
+
+Force obeys sensors, heat, geometry, communications, ammunition, collateral, surrender, and recovery.
+
+## Carrier civilization stack
+
+Protectorate ships inherit multirole carrier physics, residents, harbors, fabrication, damage, ownership, and command limits.
+
+## Multiplayer truth
+
+Mission scope, authority, orders, unit state, refusal, cargo, casualties, and evidence require authoritative consistency.
+
+## Symthaea direction
+
+Symthaea may compress, surface, pace, and compose real pressures. It may not fabricate crises, loyalty, capability, supplies, or consent.
+
+# Scale Transitions
+
+```text
+individual responder
+↕ local team
+↕ chartered unit
+↕ regional compact
+↕ temporary coalition
+↕ strategic federation
+```
+
+Each transition preserves named people, obligations, evidence, authority, and material accounting.
+
+# Unit State Flow
+
+```text
+founding event
++ charter and constituency
++ capability evidence
++ current personnel and equipment
++ recognition graph
++ service and accountability history
+= current institutional state
+```
+
+# Coalition State Flow
+
+```text
+incident claims
++ mission purpose
++ accepted unit commitments
++ delegated authority graph
++ logistics plan
++ communications conditions
++ civilian protections
+= current coalition
+```
+
+# First Implementation Dependency Order
+
+1. unit identity and charter;
+2. capability and readiness evidence;
+3. one local mobilization with household cost;
+4. two-unit interoperability;
+5. delegated command and one professional refusal;
+6. logistics, rescue, and demobilization;
+7. one small Protectorate convoy;
+8. civilian review and institutional repair;
+9. strategic compression and 500-unit operation;
+10. 20,000-unit benchmark.
+
+# Production Boundary
+
+The first Protectorate proof should attach to a playable settlement, convoy, and mobile home. System-scale federation work may prototype in isolation but must not displace Firstlight, Continuance, professions, or ordinary civilian life.
+
+## Map Maxim
+
+> **Protection becomes one system only when force, care, logistics, law, culture, and recovery remain causally connected from the individual responder to the strategic federation.**
